  "worked_solution": "Since we are given that ${a}_{4}=108$ and  $r=\\frac{3}{2}$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{4-1} \\\\ 108 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3} \\\\ 108 & =\\frac{27}{8}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,984.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >1\\,984 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >62 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}\u2061{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}\u2061(62) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}\u2061(\\frac{3}{2}) & >\\operatorname{log}\u2061(62) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(62)}{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(62)}{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(\\frac{3}{2})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(62)}{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(\\frac{3}{2})}+1\u224811.179\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,984.$",
